keywords: ["cellular automata", "probabilistic cellular automata", "noise", "ergodicity", "undecidability", "halting problem", "Gibbs measures", "Ising model", "disorder line", "correlation functions", "noise diagram", "phase diagrams", "exponential weak mixing", "effectively open sets"]
abstract: "Which temperature sets can one finite magnet realize through its correlations? On [87/4, 45/2], a zero-field Ising model with exchanges in {-1,+1} has a sampled spin correlation that changes, at a right-computably enumerable inverse temperature of halting degree, from positive strictly decreasing decay to an asymptotically damped cosine of period three in units of a fixed vector d. Its nonplanar degree-three graph has 32 vertices per geometric cell, about 4.7 x 10^22 spins per signed repeat, and |d| ~ 1.8 x 10^10 lattice spacings. One Gibbs state, exponential weak mixing and analytic pressure persist throughout the window. Every fixed-lag correlation is computable, but no computable lag or signal guarantee follows from a prescribed distance above the cut, and no sampled correlation in the window is negative before lag 10^329. More generally, for every relatively effectively open subset W of a positive rational window, a periodic +/-1 sign pattern on this fixed geometry, obtained effectively from an enumeration of W, makes W the oscillatory set of a sampled spin correlation; the converse assumes uniqueness and the all-lag sign promise. For finite rational magnets, negative point-field response somewhere has exactly the relatively effectively open diagrams under uniqueness. These results complement a noise diagram on the line, a mixing/order diagram on the square lattice, effective openness of mixing sets, their sequential realization, and exact realization of explicit semialgebraic diagrams. Realizing every relatively effectively open mixing set by one magnet with opposite order on its complement remains open."
